\title{Macroeconomics II\\
Variational methods (Soviet)}
\author{Prof. George McCandless}
\maketitle

\section{Variational methods}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Synthisis \ of Variational methods}

\begin{itemize}
\item Method of optimal control developed by the Soviets in the 1950's

\item Breaks infinite horizon problem into one involving three periods

\begin{itemize}
\item a function in time t-1, t, and t+1
\end{itemize}

\item Assume you \ know what the values were at time t-1

\item Assume you know what you want the value to be at time t+1

\item solve for optimal behavior at time t

\item if you want a stationary state, let $x_{t-1}=x_{t}=x_{t+1}=x$ and
solve for the $x$'s that work

\item Non-stationary states can be found

\item Have beginning t=0 values and final t=N values (N is number of periods
you are going to have (years, days, minutes, depends on the problem)

\item Choose some initial path between start = $x_{0}$ and finish = $x_{N}$

\item Use $x_{0}$ and $x_{2}$ to find a new $x_{1}^{\ast }$, then use $%
x_{1}^{\ast }$ and $x_{3}$ to find a new $x_{2}^{\ast }$, continue to $x_{N}$

\item repeat backwards \ (from $x_{N}$\ to $x_{0}$) and forwards until path
converges
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Robinson Crusoe economy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Only one agent (who we call Robinson Crusoe) with utility%
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty }\beta ^{i}u(c_{t+i}) 
\]

\item Notice that it is a discounted infinite horizon utility (and here no
labor decision, could be)

\item Budget constaints%
\[
k_{t+1}=(1-\delta )k_{t}+i_{t}
\]%
and%
\[
y_{t}=f(k_{t})\geq c_{t}+i_{t}
\]

\item 
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Variational methods}

\QTR{framesubtitle}{Solving for a stationary state}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Assume that values for endogenous variables for periods $s-1$ and $s+1$
are known

\item Solve for values in period $s$

\item For a stationary state, use solution for $s$ to find

\begin{itemize}
\item Find equilibrium where values for $s-1$, $s$, and $s+1$ are the same

\item This is a stationary state that meets the equilibrium conditions in
every period
\end{itemize}

\item Stationary states are not the only use of Variational methods, we use
it to get first order conditions for approximate models that we will use
later

\item Also, if the values at starting and end points are known, variational
methods can be used to solve for the optimal path between those points by
choosing an initial (probably wrong) path and then start from that path to
successively find better ones (which converge to the optimal path
\end{enumerate}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{General framework}

\begin{itemize}
\item Optimization problem: ($F\left( x_{t+i},x_{t+1+i}\right) $ is the
objective function)%
\[
\max_{\left\{ x_{x}\right\} _{s=t}^{\infty }}\sum_{i=0}^{\infty }\beta
^{i}F\left( x_{t+i},x_{t+1+i}\right) . 
\]

\item The Euler condition (a necessary condition) 
\[
0=F_{2}\left( x_{s-1},x_{s}\right) +\beta F_{1}\left( x_{s},x_{s+1}\right) 
\]

\item Note that the Euler condition has $x_{s-1}$, $x_{s}$ and $x_{s+1}$ as
variables. \ Can solve for $x_{s}$ as a function of $x_{s-1}$ and $x_{s+1}$.

\item Transversality condition 
\[
\lim_{s\rightarrow \infty }\beta ^{s}F_{1}\left( x_{s},x_{s+1}\right)
x_{s}=0. 
\]
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Example RC\ economy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Substitute budget constraints into utility function. \ Get:%
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty }\beta ^{i}u(f(k_{t+i})-k_{t+i+1}+(1-\delta )k_{t+i}) 
\]

\item Note each objective function is a function of $k_{t+i}$ and $k_{t+i+1}$

\item First order conditions are%
\begin{eqnarray*}
0 &=&\beta ^{s-t}u^{\prime }(f(k_{s})-k_{s+1}+(1-\delta )k_{s})\left(
f^{\prime }(k_{s})+(1-\delta )\right)  \\
&&-\beta ^{s-t-1}u^{\prime }(f(k_{s-1})-k_{s}+(1-\delta )k_{s-1}).
\end{eqnarray*}

\item Notice that the first order conditions are functions of $k$ at times $%
s-1$, $s$, and $s+1$.
\end{enumerate}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Example economy (continued)}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Which can be written as the Euler equation (a necessary condition)%
\[
f^{\prime }(k_{s})+(1-\delta )=\frac{u^{\prime }(f(k_{s-1})-k_{s}+(1-\delta
)k_{s-1})}{\beta u^{\prime }(f(k_{s})-k_{s+1}+(1-\delta )k_{s})}. 
\]

\item In a stationary state, $k_{s+1}=k_{s}=k_{s-1}=\overline{k}$, so the
above becomes%
\[
f^{\prime }(\overline{k})=\frac{1}{\beta }-1+\delta 
\]%
The value of $\overline{k}$ is implicitly defined

\item Savings in a stationary state is $\overline{s}=\delta \overline{k}$
\end{enumerate}

\begin{itemize}
\item See book for Robinson Crusoe with labor decision included
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Transversality condition}

\begin{itemize}
\item Transversality condition for example economy%
\[
\lim_{i\rightarrow \infty }\beta ^{i}u^{\prime }\left(
f(k_{t+i})-k_{t+i+1}+(1-\delta )k_{t+i}\right) \left( f^{\prime
}(k_{t+i})+(1-\delta )\right) k_{t+i}=0. 
\]

\item In a stationary state with $\overline{k}>0$ 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\lim_{i\rightarrow \infty }\beta ^{i}u^{\prime }\left( f(\overline{k}%
)-\delta \overline{k}\right) \left( f^{\prime }(\overline{k})+(1-\delta
)\right) \overline{k} \\
&=&\lim_{i\rightarrow \infty }\beta ^{i}u^{\prime }\left( f(\overline{k}%
)-\delta \overline{k}\right) \left( \frac{1}{\beta }-1+\delta +(1-\delta
)\right) \overline{k} \\
&=&\lim_{i\rightarrow \infty }\beta ^{i-1}u^{\prime }\left( f(\overline{k}%
)-\delta \overline{k}\right) \overline{k}\rightarrow 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\item because $u^{\prime }\left( f(\overline{k})-\delta \overline{k}\right) 
\overline{k}$ is a finite constant for $\overline{k}>0$
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\subsection{Market economy}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Competitive market economy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Many agents

\begin{itemize}
\item Households

\item Firms
\end{itemize}

\item Markets for goods, labor, and rental of capital

\item Individual take aggregate values as given

\item Decentralized decision making
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Competitive market economy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Unit mass of agents

\begin{itemize}
\item indexed from $0$ to $1$ continuously
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\[
H_t=\int_0^1h_t^idi\text{ and }K_t=\int_0^1k_t^idi 
\]

\begin{itemize}
\item Include individual labor supply decision%
\[
u\left( c_{t}^{i},l_{t}^{i}\right) =u\left( c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) 
\]

\item subject to the budget constaints 
\[
c_{t}^{i}=w_{t}h_{t}^{i}+r_{t}k_{t}^{i}+i_{t}^{i} 
\]%
\[
k_{t+1}^{i}=\left( 1-\delta \right) k_{t}^{i}+i_{t}^{i} 
\]%
\[
w_{t}=f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) 
\]%
\[
r_{t}=f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) 
\]
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Set up problem as Legrange problem }

\begin{eqnarray*}
L &=&\sum_{t=0}^{\infty }\beta ^{t}\left[ u\left(
c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) -\lambda _{t}^{1}\left( k_{t+1}^{i}-\left(
1-\delta \right) k_{t}^{i}-i_{t}^{i}\right) \right. \\
&&\left. -\lambda _{t}^{2}\left( f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right)
h_{t}^{i}+f_{k}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right)
k_{t}^{i}-c_{t}^{i}-i_{t}^{i}\right) \right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\begin{itemize}
\item Difference between $h_{t}^{i}$ and $H_{t}$ and $k_{t}^{i}$ and $K_{t}$

\item $H_{t}$ and $K_{t}$ are the economy wide (aggregate) values \ of labor
and capital

\item In optimization, $H_{t}$ and $K_{t}$ are considered given

\item Equilibrium conditions are applied after optimization
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{First order conditions}

\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial c_{t}^{i}} &=&u_{c}\left(
c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) +\lambda _{t}^{2}=0 \\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial h_{t}^{i}} &=&-u_{h}\left(
c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) -\lambda _{t}^{2}f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right)
=0 \\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial k_{t+1}^{i}} &=&-\lambda _{t}^{1}+\beta \lambda
_{t+1}^{1}\left( 1-\delta \right) -\beta \lambda _{t+1}^{2}f_{k}\left(
K_{t+1},H_{t+1}\right) =0 \\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial i_{t}^{i}} &=&\lambda _{t}^{1}+\lambda _{t}^{2}=0
\end{eqnarray*}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{First order conditions (continued)}

These simplify to 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{c}\left( c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) &=&\lambda _{t}^{1}=-\lambda
_{t}^{2} \\
\frac{u_{h}\left( c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) }{u_{c}\left(
c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) } &=&f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) \\
\frac{u_{c}\left( c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) }{u_{c}\left(
c_{t+1}^{i},1-h_{t+1}^{i}\right) } &=&\beta \left[ f_{k}\left(
K_{t+1},H_{t+1}\right) +\left( 1-\delta \right) \right]
\end{eqnarray*}%
For the individual decisions we need to include the budget constraint 
\[
k_{t+1}^{i}=\left( 1-\delta \right) k_{t}^{i}+f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right)
h_{t}^{i}+f_{k}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) k_{t}^{i}-c_{t}^{i} 
\]

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Solving the model }

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{After and only after} the individual decision rules are found,
can one aggregate across individuals

\begin{itemize}
\item Because the $K_{t}$ and $H_{t}$ are not controled by individuals
\end{itemize}

\item These are found from the equilibrium conditions%
\[
K_{t}=\int_{0}^{1}k_{t}^{i}di 
\]%
\[
H_{t}=\int_{0}^{1}h_{t}^{i}di 
\]

\item Aggregation condition for consumption%
\[
C_{t}=\int_{0}^{1}c_{t}^{i}di 
\]
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Solving the model}

\begin{itemize}
\item Given that production is homogenous of degree 1 
\[
f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) H_{t}+f_{k}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right)
K_{t}=f\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) 
\]

\item The conditions for equilibrium are%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{u_{h}\left( C_{t},1-H_{t}\right) }{u_{c}\left( C_{t},1-H_{t}\right) }
&=&f_{h}\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) \\
\frac{u_{c}\left( C_{t},1-H_{t}\right) }{u_{c}\left(
C_{t+1},1-H_{t+1}\right) } &=&\beta \left[ f_{k}\left(
K_{t+1},H_{t+1}\right) +\left( 1-\delta \right) \right]
\end{eqnarray*}%
and the budget constraint is 
\[
K_{t+1}=\left( 1-\delta \right) K_{t}+f\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) -C_{t} 
\]
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{The stationary states }

\begin{itemize}
\item Solving for the stationary states, one gets
\end{itemize}

\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{u_{h}\left( \overline{C},1-\overline{H}\right) }{u_{c}\left( \overline{%
C},1-\overline{H}\right) } &=&f_{h}\left( \overline{K},\overline{H}\right) \\
\frac{1}{\beta }-\left( 1-\delta \right) &=&f_{k}\left( \overline{K},%
\overline{H}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
and the budget constraint is 
\[
f\left( \overline{K},\overline{H}\right) -\delta \overline{K}=\overline{C} 
\]

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{The stationary states}

\begin{itemize}
\item Or, the two equations in $H$ and $K$ of%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{u_{h}\left( f\left( \overline{K},\overline{H}\right) -\delta \overline{%
K},1-\overline{H}\right) }{u_{c}\left( f\left( \overline{K},\overline{H}%
\right) -\delta \overline{K},1-\overline{H}\right) } &=&f_{h}\left( 
\overline{K},\overline{H}\right) \\
\frac{1}{\beta }-\left( 1-\delta \right) &=&f_{k}\left( \overline{K},%
\overline{H}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\item These are the same two conditions one gets for the Robinson Crusoe
economy (when done with labor decision)
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{The second welfare theorem}

\begin{quotation}
"The Second Fundamental Welfare Theorem. \ If household preferences and firm
productions sets are convex, there is a complete set of markets with
publicly known prices, and every agent acts as a price taker, then \textit{%
any Pareto optimal outcome can be achieved as a competitive equilibrium if
appropriate lump-sum transfers of wealth are arranged}."
\end{quotation}

\begin{itemize}
\item What does the Second Fundamental Welfare Theorem mean?

\begin{itemize}
\item A perfectly competitive economy can be solved as a social planner (one
person) problem

\item This need not work if the economy is not perfectly competitive
\end{itemize}

\item The above statement is for a finite dimension economy. \ Infinite
dimensioned economies are a bit more complicated. \ More assumptions are
needed to get competitive equilibrium. \ In particular, a transversality
condition. \ We simply assume a competitive equilibrium exists
\end{itemize}

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndFrame}{\end{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{frame}%
%EndExpansion

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginFrame}{\begin{frame}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\begin{frame}%
%EndExpansion

\QTR{frametitle}{Homework}

\begin{itemize}
\item Exercise: \ Find the stationary state equilibrium for an economy with 
\[
u\left( c_{t}^{i},1-h_{t}^{i}\right) =\log c_{t}^{i}+A\log \left(
1-h_{t}^{i}\right) , 
\]%
$A=1.72$, and%
\[
f\left( K_{t},H_{t}\right) =\gamma K_{t}^{\theta }H_{t}^{1-\theta } 
\]%
with $\gamma =3$ and $\theta =.56$. \ Use $\beta =.98$ and $\delta =.06.$ \
Find the numerical values of output, consumption, the capital stock, and
labor supply in the stationary state using Matlab. \ 
\end{itemize}

